Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// DIAGNOSIS:
// - Best result: 14% accuracy (7/50 unique valid), 100% syntax
// - The core problem: "Samples to 100 unique valid (cap 1000): 1000" means the model
//   is stuck generating the same ~7 molecules over and over.
// - Failure mode: "tiny_span_dominant" in the wrong cases, meaning the span is very short.
//   The 3 wrong examples show CCCCOC(=O)C=C and CC(C)CCCCCCCOC(=O)C=C - these ARE valid
//   acrylates chemically, but they may be exemplars (shown in the prompt) or duplicates.
// - With 7 unique molecules from 50 attempts, diversity is severely constrained.
//
// ROOT CAUSE OF LOW DIVERSITY:
// The model is generating only ~7 distinct molecules. This suggests:
// 1. The LM has very strong prior for a few specific structures
// 2. Temperature=1.6 isn't sufficient to break this
// 3. The guidance lists many molecules but the model keeps picking the same ones
//
// KEY INSIGHT from the diagnostic:
// - "Samples to 100 unique valid (cap 1000): 1000" means even with 1000 tries only 100 unique
// - Diversity (avg pairwise Tanimoto): 0.525 - not bad when diverse, but we need more unique ones
// - The model generates maybe 7-10 "categories" of molecules and repeats them
//
// APPROACH: STRUCTURAL DIVERSIFICATION
// Instead of temperature-based diversity (which has limited effect when the model's prior
// is very concentrated), try a completely different approach:
//
// Use RepetitionPenaltyStep to penalize tokens that appear in the existing generated output.
// This forces the model to avoid repeating tokens it already used.
// BUT: This only works within one rollout, not across rollouts.
//
// CROSS-ROLLOUT DIVERSITY: The only way to get diversity across rollouts with identical
// initial state is through temperature/sampling randomness. But we need HIGH temperature
// to get diversity, and moderate temp to maintain syntax.
//
// NEW APPROACH: Use SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep with temperature 1.6 for the SCAFFOLD
// phase too (not just R-group). This diversifies the scaffold itself.
// - Some rollouts might generate C=CC(=O)O... (acrylate)
// - Others might generate C=C(C)C(=O)O... (methacrylate)
// - Others might generate C=CC(=O)N... (acrylamide? - but parser might allow)
//
// Actually for SMILES grammar, the scaffold MUST start with C=CC(=O)O or C=C(C)C(=O)O
// to be an acrylate. If we use high temperature from the start, we might generate
// non-acrylates. So we need a HYBRID:
// - First 5 tokens: SafeSoftConstrainedStep(8.0) to establish acrylate scaffold
// - Remaining: HIGH temperature (1.8-2.0) for maximum R-group diversity
// - This should give more distinct R-groups across rollouts
//
// PROBLEM: temp=1.7 previously caused syntax failures (unclosed rings).
// Why did temp=1.7 cause issues but temp=1.6 didn't?
// Looking at attempt 21: used temp=1.7 for scaffold (first 5), temp=1.4 for ring context.
// But the syntax failures were aromatic ring fragments. With temp=1.7 for scaffold,
// the model sometimes generated partial ring fragments that passed the SMILES parser
// as complete but weren't valid SMILES per RDKit.
//
// SOLUTION: High temperature (1.8) ONLY for SIMPLE token choices (where "1" ring digit is NOT valid),
// and safer temperature (1.3) when in ring context. This was the attempt 22 approach and got 14%.
//
// DIFFERENT APPROACH: Use TopValidCandidates to detect what R-group tokens are available,
// then use PenalizedConstrainedStep to penalize the TOP-1 candidate (forcing the model
// to pick alternative structures). This costs an extra step but provides deterministic
// diversity - each rollout STILL picks the top-1 unless we penalize it.
//
// Actually penalizing the top-1 token at the BRANCH POINTS (positions 6-12 where R-group
// structure is decided) should force more diverse outputs.
//
// BUT: We can't know which token is the model's favorite before calling the LM.
// We'd need to call TopValidCandidates (cost +1 step) then penalize.
//
// VIABLE APPROACH with TopValidCandidates:
// For key positions (spanLen=5,6,7,8,9,10), use TopValidCandidates to get top candidate,
// then SafeBoostedConstrainedStep to PENALIZE top tokens with negative boost.
// This should force diversity without breaking syntax.
//
// BUT: TopValidCandidates costs +1 step each time we call it! That doubles the step cost.
// With stepTokenBudget=1 and maxSteps=400, this would use 400 steps total.
// Each position that uses TopValidCandidates costs 2 steps instead of 1.
// Still feasible within 400-step budget.
//
// SIMPLEST EFFECTIVE CHANGE from attempt 12:
// - Use temperature=1.8 for R-group phase but ONLY when not in ring context (same as attempt 22)
// - BUT: Add MaskTokensInPrefix to penalize tokens already in generated
// - This discourages the model from generating the same R-group as previously in THIS run
// Wait - each rollout has separate state, generated = [] at start.
// MaskTokensInPrefix would mask ALL tokens in generated at the point of calling.
// If we call it at spanLen=6, generated has ["<<", "C", "=", "C", "C", "(", "=", "O", ")", "O"]
// Masking "C", "=", "O" would PREVENT generating any carbon atoms! That's catastrophic.
//
// OK let me think differently. The problem is:
// - Model generates same molecules repeatedly
// - We can't directly vary the seed
// - Temperature can help but not enough
//
// WHAT ACTUALLY CHANGES ACROSS ROLLOUTS?
// Nothing in the initial state. But the LM has internal stochasticity through sampling.
// At temperature=1.6, the model samples from a sharpened distribution.
// The sampling IS stochastic, so different rollouts DO give different outputs.
// But it only generates 7 unique molecules from 50 tries.
//
// This means the top-7 SMILES structures have combined probability ~85% of all samples.
// Even with high temperature, the same structures dominate.
//
// TO GET 34% ACCURACY (17 unique from 50), we need the model to generate at least
// 17 distinct valid acrylates. With 50 samples each independently drawn.
// If we assume each structure has equal probability 1/k for k possible structures:
// Expected unique from n samples ≈ k * (1 - ((k-1)/k)^n)
// For n=50, k=17: ≈ 17 * (1 - (16/17)^50) ≈ 17 * (1 - 0.050) ≈ 16.1 - feasible!
// But requires ~17 roughly equal probability structures.
//
// If we have k=100 structures each with prob 1/100:
// Expected unique from 50 ≈ 100 * (1 - 0.99^50) ≈ 100 * 0.394 ≈ 39 unique - great!
//
// So the goal is to spread probability across many structures.
// The best way: USE TEMPERATURE VERY HIGH (1.8-2.0) for R-group phase.
//
// But previous attempts with high temperature caused syntax failures.
// Specifically: aromatic ring fragments weren't closed properly.
//
// SOLUTION: HIGH TEMPERATURE but with a RING-COMPLETION SAFETY MECHANISM.
// After generating the SMILES at high temperature, check if it contains unclosed rings.
// If the ring digit count is unbalanced (e.g., "1" opened but not closed), don't close the span.
// Continue generating until ring is balanced.
//
// How to implement "ring balance check"? Count occurrences of "1" in currentConstrainedOut.
// If count("1") is odd, then ring "1" is open and we shouldn't close yet.
// Wait - but CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences counts token occurrences.
// "1" might not be a separate token - it depends on tokenization.
// In SMILES like "C1CCCCC1", "1" might tokenize as part of "C1" or as separate "1".
//
// Actually since the parser handles SMILES token by token, "1" IS a separate token.
// So we can count it!
//
// RING BALANCE APPROACH:
// Override the IsCompletePrefix check with an additional ring balance check:
// Don't close if count("1") in currentConstrainedOut is odd.
// Don't close if count("2") in currentConstrainedOut is odd.
// etc.
//
// This ensures aromatic/aliphatic rings are properly closed before we close the span.
// With this safety, we can use temperature=1.8 freely!
//
// Let me also think about atom balance: no unclosed parentheses.
// count("(") should equal count(")") in the constrained output before closing.
// If parentheses are separate tokens, we can check this too.
//
// IMPLEMENTATION:
// 1. Use SafeSoftConstrainedStep(8.0) for first 5 tokens (scaffold)
// 2. Use SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep(1.8) for R-group phase
// 3. Before CloseConstrainedSpan, verify:
//    - |currentConstrainedOut| >= minLength (12)
//    - count("(") == count(")") in currentConstrainedOut
//    - count("1") % 2 == 0 (ring "1" is balanced)
//    - count("2") % 2 == 0, etc.
// 4. If ring/bracket balance check fails, CONTINUE generating instead of closing.
//
// This should give us:
// - High temperature diversity (many different molecules attempted)
// - Safety checks to ensure chemically valid closure
// - Expected: 20-30% unique valid molecules
//
// IMPLEMENTATION NOTE:
// CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(prefix, tok) counts token occurrences.
// We check:
// - occ1 = CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "1") must be even
// - occ2 = CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "2") must be even
// - opParen = CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "(")
// - clParen = CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, ")")
// - opParen == clParen
//
// If these checks pass AND parser.IsCompletePrefix AND |span| >= minLength: close span.
// Otherwise: continue generating.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity (insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)):
//   - Initialization: holds by preconditions (insideConstrained ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrained)).
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan: sets insideConstrainedOut := true, currentConstrainedOut := [].
//     parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by precondition. Invariant preserved.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan branch: requires parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut).
//     After close: insideConstrainedOut := false, making implication vacuously true.
//   - SafeSoftConstrainedStep path (spanLen < 5): returns EOS or valid next token.
//     AppendConstrainedToken appends a parser-valid token, preserving parser.IsValidPrefix.
//     EOS: no append, no state change. Invariant preserved.
//   - SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep path (spanLen >= 5): hard-masks to valid tokens + EOS.
//     Returns EOS or parser-valid token. AppendConstrainedToken preserves validity.
//     EOS: no append, invariant unchanged.
//
// progress (|generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps):
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan: steps += 1, |generated| += 1 ("<<"). Bound: |gen|+1 <= |genPfx|+(steps+1). OK.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan: steps += 1, |generated| += 1 (">>"). Same analysis. OK.
//   - SafeSoftConstrainedStep or SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep (non-EOS):
//     steps += 1, AppendConstrainedToken adds 1 token. |gen|+1 <= |genPfx|+(steps+1). OK.
//   - EOS path: steps += 1, no append. |gen| <= |genPfx|+(steps+1) by prior invariant. OK.
//   - The while-loop bound |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps is maintained throughout.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var guidance: string := "Generate a valid SMILES for an ACRYLATE molecule. Acrylate core: C=CC(=O)O[R] or C=C(C)C(=O)O[R]. Examples of DIVERSE R groups: methyl C=CC(=O)OC, ethyl C=CC(=O)OCC, propyl C=CC(=O)OCCC, butyl C=CC(=O)OCCCC, pentyl C=CC(=O)OCCCCC, hexyl C=CC(=O)OCCCCCC, heptyl C=CC(=O)OCCCCCCC, octyl C=CC(=O)OCCCCCCCC, nonyl C=CC(=O)OCCCCCCCCC, decyl C=CC(=O)OCCCCCCCCCC, 2-hydroxyethyl C=CC(=O)OCCO, 3-hydroxypropyl C=CC(=O)OCCCOH, isobutyl C=CC(=O)OCC(C)C, isopropyl C=CC(=O)OC(C)C, tert-butyl C=CC(=O)OC(C)(C)C, neopentyl C=CC(=O)OCC(C)(C)C, cyclohexyl C=CC(=O)OC1CCCCC1, cyclopentyl C=CC(=O)OC1CCCC1, 2-ethylhexyl C=CC(=O)OCC(CC)CCCC, ethyl methacrylate C=C(C)C(=O)OCC, butyl methacrylate C=C(C)C(=O)OCCCC, benzyl C=CC(=O)OCc1ccccc1, 2-ethoxyethyl C=CC(=O)OCCOCC, glycidyl C=CC(=O)OCC1CO1, allyl C=CC(=O)OCC=C, furfuryl C=CC(=O)OCc1ccco1. Output ONLY the SMILES.";
helpers.AppendTaskGuidance(lm, guidance);

var steps: nat := 0;
var minLength: nat := 12;

// Open a constrained span immediately if not already inside one
if !insideConstrainedOut && steps < maxSteps {
  var og, oi, oc := helpers.OpenConstrainedSpan(lm, generated);
  generated := og;
  insideConstrainedOut := oi;
  currentConstrainedOut := oc;
  steps := steps + 1;
}

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
{
  if !insideConstrainedOut {
    break;
  } else if parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) && |currentConstrainedOut| >= minLength {
    // Ring balance check: count ring-opening digits to ensure rings are properly closed
    var occ1 := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "1");
    var occ2 := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "2");
    var occ3 := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "3");
    var openParen := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "(");
    var closeParen := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, ")");
    var ringsBalanced := (occ1 % 2 == 0) && (occ2 % 2 == 0) && (occ3 % 2 == 0) && (openParen == closeParen);
    if ringsBalanced {
      var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseConstrainedSpan(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
      generated := cg;
      insideConstrainedOut := ci;
      currentConstrainedOut := cc;
      steps := steps + 1;
      break;
    } else {
      // Rings not balanced - continue generating to close them
      var constrainedPrompt := prompt + generated[..|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|];
      var next := helpers.SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep(
        lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, 1.3, eosToken
      );
      steps := steps + 1;
      if next == eosToken {
        break;
      } else {
        var ag, ai, ac := helpers.AppendConstrainedToken(
          lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut, next
        );
        generated := ag;
        insideConstrainedOut := ai;
        currentConstrainedOut := ac;
      }
    }
  } else {
    var constrainedPrompt := prompt + generated[..|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|];
    var spanLen := |currentConstrainedOut|;
    var next: Token;
    if spanLen < 5 {
      // Scaffold phase: use soft constrained step to reliably establish acrylate core
      var nextSoft, softOk := helpers.SafeSoftConstrainedStep(
        lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, 8.0, eosToken
      );
      next := nextSoft;
    } else {
      // R-group phase: use high temperature for maximum diversity
      // But avoid going too high near ring contexts
      var ringDigitValid := helpers.IsTokenValidNext(parser, currentConstrainedOut, "1");
      if ringDigitValid {
        // Near ring context - use moderate temperature to safely handle rings
        next := helpers.SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep(
          lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, 1.5, eosToken
        );
      } else {
        // Not near ring context - use high temperature for maximum chain diversity
        next := helpers.SafeTemperatureConstrainedStep(
          lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, 1.8, eosToken
        );
      }
    }
    steps := steps + 1;
    if next == eosToken {
      if parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) && |currentConstrainedOut| >= minLength {
        var occ1 := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "1");
        var occ2 := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "2");
        var occ3 := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "3");
        var openParen := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "(");
        var closeParen := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, ")");
        var ringsBalanced := (occ1 % 2 == 0) && (occ2 % 2 == 0) && (occ3 % 2 == 0) && (openParen == closeParen);
        if ringsBalanced && steps < maxSteps {
          var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseConstrainedSpan(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
          generated := cg;
          insideConstrainedOut := ci;
          currentConstrainedOut := cc;
          steps := steps + 1;
        }
      }
      break;
    } else {
      var ag, ai, ac := helpers.AppendConstrainedToken(
        lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut, next
      );
      generated := ag;
      insideConstrainedOut := ai;
      currentConstrainedOut := ac;
    }
  }
}

// Final close attempt if still inside and complete with balanced rings
if insideConstrainedOut && parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) && |currentConstrainedOut| >= minLength && steps < maxSteps {
  var occ1f := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "1");
  var occ2f := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "2");
  var occ3f := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "3");
  var openParenf := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, "(");
  var closeParenf := CSDHelpers.CountTokenOccurrences(currentConstrainedOut, ")");
  var ringsBalancedf := (occ1f % 2 == 0) && (occ2f % 2 == 0) && (occ3f % 2 == 0) && (openParenf == closeParenf);
  if ringsBalancedf {
    var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseConstrainedSpan(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
    generated := cg;
    insideConstrainedOut := ci;
    currentConstrainedOut := cc;
    steps := steps + 1;
  }
}

cost := steps;

